session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr641 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:473 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-09-04T14:49:33Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-09-06T12:03:02Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr641Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #473 was deferred from #390/#568: those PRs gave each dataset exactly one combined IncrementalReasoner (a flat ruleset, full-replace-only via POST /{dataset}/rules), explicitly leaving 'per-ruleset-scoped named add/delete with an id' as a follow-up because it needed a HashMap<RulesetId, Vec<Rule>> per dataset plus a rule-identity/dedup story for a rule shared across two named rulesets. Investigation of the merged #568 rules_endpoint.rs found apply_ruleset_diff (diffs a target combined ruleset against the live reasoner's active_rules(), applies the delta via datalog::IncrementalReasoner's apply_rule_insertions/apply_rule_deletions, falling back to a full rebuild on NotStratifiable/contradiction) — reusable as-is for the new endpoints rather than needing new datalog-crate primitives. The key design move: DatasetEntry (registry.rs) gains a `rulesets: RulesetMap` (ruleset-id -> its exact rules) as bookkeeping separate from the live reasoner. Both POST /{dataset}/rules/{id} and DELETE /{dataset}/rules/{id} work by computing 'what should the combined live ruleset be' (union of every *other* id's rules, deduplicated by Rule value-equality, plus/minus this id's own rules) and handing that combined target to the existing apply_ruleset_diff via a new apply_combined_ruleset dispatcher — this is what makes a rule shared between two named rulesets survive deleting one of them for free: it's still present in the recomputed union via the surviving id, so the diff against the live reasoner sees no change for that rule. The plain no-id POST /{dataset}/rules (#390/#568) keeps its existing full-replace behavior unchanged but now also clears the rulesets map, since a full replace discards all previously-loaded rules regardless of source and no named id can meaningfully survive it. TDD: 8 new integration tests in tests/ruleset_scoped_delete.rs (all written and confirmed red before implementation, then unignored together once the implementation was written) covering creation, scoped deletion, cross-ruleset rule-sharing survival, both 404 cases, read-only 403, same-id replace, and the plain-POST-clears-named-rulesets interaction; all passed on first implementation attempt, and the pre-existing runtime_ruleset.rs (7 tests) and rules_endpoint unit tests (9 tests) continued to pass unmodified." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:641 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr641Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr641 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr641Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Computed the combined ruleset (union of surviving/other ids' rules, deduplicated by Rule value-equality) and handed it to the existing #568 apply_ruleset_diff, rather than building a new datalog-crate primitive that directly retracts 'this id's rules unless referenced elsewhere'." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered adding a reference-counted or ruleset-id-aware variant of apply_rule_deletions/apply_rule_insertions inside the datalog crate itself, so the sparql_endpoint layer wouldn't need to recompute a full combined-ruleset diff on every id-scoped call. Rejected: the existing diff_rulesets/apply_ruleset_diff machinery from #568 already solves 'move the live reasoner from ruleset A to ruleset B soundly, incrementally where possible, falling back to a full rebuild otherwise' — reusing it for 'B = A with one named subset swapped out' is a pure sparql_endpoint-layer computation (set union/diff over Vec<Rule>, cheap relative to actual materialisation) with zero changes needed in datalog/src/incremental.rs, and keeps the ruleset-id concept entirely out of the datalog crate, which has no notion of 'named rulesets' and shouldn't need one." .
